   Calixto-Hope G. and Bettegowda, Chetan and Levine, Stuart S. and Bale,
   Tejus A. and Brennan, Cameron and Reardon, David A. and Lang, Frederick
   F. and Chiocca, E. Antonio and Ligon, Keith L. and White, Forest M. and
   Sharma, Padmanee and Tabar, Viviane and Agar, Nathalie Y. R. and GBMTeam
   Lab},
Title = {Investigative needle core biopsies support multimodal deep-data
   generation in glioblastoma},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {16},
Number = {1},
Month = {APR 28},
Abstract = {Glioblastoma (GBM) is an aggressive primary brain cancer with few
   effective therapies. Stereotactic needle biopsies are routinely used for
   diagnosis; however, the feasibility and utility of investigative
   biopsies to monitor treatment response remains ill-defined. Here, we
   demonstrate the depth of data generation possible from routine
   stereotactic needle core biopsies and perform highly resolved
   multi-omics analyses, including single-cell RNA sequencing, spatial
   transcriptomics, metabolomics, proteomics, phosphoproteomics, T-cell
   clonotype analysis, and MHC Class I immunopeptidomics on standard biopsy
   tissue obtained intra-operatively. We also examine biopsies taken from
   different locations and provide a framework for measuring spatial and
   genomic heterogeneity. Finally, we investigate the utility of
   stereotactic biopsies as a method for generating patient-derived
   xenograft (PDX) models. Multimodal dataset integration highlights
   spatially mapped immune cell-associated metabolic pathways and validates
   inferred cell-cell ligand-receptor interactions. In conclusion,
   investigative biopsies provide data-rich insight into disease processes
   and may be useful in evaluating treatment responses.},
