Abstract = {Urban markets in African cities exhibit a complex interplay of six
   characteristics, namely physical form, social interactions, economic
   activities, governance structures, cultural values and spatial
   contestations. These unique characteristics are being disrupted due to
   the growing penetration of neoliberal urbanism coupled with the desire
   of municipal authorities to redevelop markets to resemble western-style
   shopping malls. Drawing on notions of modernity and qualitative
   interviews with 60 study participants across three major Ghanaian urban
   markets in different cities - Kumasi's Kejetia market, Cape Coast's
   Kotokuraba market and Ho's Central market - this paper explores how
   market redevelopment is changing the character of African urban markets.
   Findings reveal a gradual erosion of four key attributes - physical,
   social, economic and governance - following redevelopment, resulting in
   a transformation that mirrors the characteristics of shopping malls. We
   conceptualize the emerging transformation as a shift from markets to
   mal-kets. This transformation prompts critical rethinking of urban
   policy to ensure that contemporary market designs incorporate indigenous
   knowledge and adequately preserve the distinctive qualities of African
   urban markets.},
